

⭐ THE ATOMIC GRAMMAR (CMB‑ENC v0.1)

The grammar has five atomic families, each with subtypes.Every atom is a first‑class object in the blueprint language.

⭐ 1. Bit Operations

These are the smallest possible atoms — the “quarks” of encryption.

XOR — fundamental mixing primitive

AND / OR / NOT — boolean logic

SHIFT_L / SHIFT_R — directional bit movement

ROT_L / ROT_R — circular rotation

BIT_PERMUTE(pattern) — arbitrary bit reordering

BIT_SLICE(mask) — extract structured subsets of bits

These atoms define the bit‑level physics of your universe.

⭐ 2. Word Operations

These atoms operate on 16‑, 32‑, 64‑, or 128‑bit words.

ADD_MOD(2^n) — ARX‑style addition

SUB_MOD(2^n)

MUL_MOD(2^n)

XOR_WORD

ROT_WORD(k)

WORD_PERMUTE(pattern)

These atoms give you the ChaCha / Salsa / ARX family for free.

⭐ 3. Nonlinear Atoms

These are the atoms that create confusion, nonlinearity, and resistance to linear/ differential attacks.

SBOX(n→m) — substitution box

INVERSE_MOD(p) — modular inversion

EXP_MOD(p) — exponentiation

LUT(table_id) — lookup table

POLY_MAP(coeffs) — polynomial mapping

These atoms are the soul of symmetric cryptography.

⭐ 4. Algebraic Structure Atoms

These atoms define the mathematical universe your cipher lives in.

GF(2^n)_ADD / MUL

RING_Zn_ADD / MUL

GROUP_OP(G) — generic group operation

SCALAR_MULT(curve) — ECC‑like

LATTICE_MAP(dim, basis) — PQC‑like

VECTOR_OP(dim)

These atoms allow your grammar to express:

RSA‑like

ECC‑like

Lattice‑like

Code‑based

Multivariate

…all from the same substrate.

⭐ 5. Randomness & State Atoms

These atoms define entropy, state evolution, and time.

RNG(seed_policy)

DRBG(mode)

NONCE(policy)

COUNTER(width)

STATE_SPLIT / STATE_MERGE

ABSORB / SQUEEZE (sponge atoms)

These atoms give you:

stream ciphers

sponge constructions

AEAD modes

PRNGs

KDFs

…all as natural consequences of the grammar.

⭐ Atomic Grammar Syntax (CMB‑ENC)

Every atom has the same structural form:

atom <NAME> {
  family: <bit | word | nonlinear | algebraic | state>;
  type: <specific subtype>;
  params: { ... };
}

Example:

atom SBOX_CMB {
  family: nonlinear;
  type: sbox;
  params: {
    in: 8;
    out: 8;
    table: "cmb_sbox_profile_1";
  }
}

This is the DNA of your encryption universe.

⭐ How Atoms Combine (Grammar Rules)

Atoms combine into steps, steps into rounds, rounds into constructions, constructions into schemes.

Step rule:

step ::= atom | atom(atom) | atom ⊕ atom | atom → atom

Round rule:

round ::= step*

Construction rule:

construction ::= round* | sponge | feistel | spn | arx

Scheme rule:

scheme ::= construction + mode + key_schedule + profile

This is the formal grammar that makes your engine possible.

